Article 1: Where a shareholder individually or collectively holding more than ten percent of the total voting rights of all shareholders of a company brings a lawsuit for dissolution of the company on any of the following grounds and meets the requirements of Article 182 of the Company Law, the People's Court shall accept the case:
(1) The company has failed to convene a shareholders' meeting or a general meeting of shareholders for more than two consecutive years, causing serious difficulties in the company's operation and management;
(2) The shareholders are unable to reach the proportion required by law or the company's articles of association when voting, and have been unable to make a valid resolution of the shareholders' meeting or general meeting of shareholders for more than two consecutive years, causing serious difficulties in the company's operation and management;
(3) There has been long-term conflict among the company's directors, and the conflict cannot be resolved through the shareholders' meeting or general meeting of shareholders, causing serious difficulties in the company's operation and management;
(4) Other serious difficulties in operation and management occur, and the continued existence of the company will cause significant losses to the interests of shareholders.
Where a shareholder brings a lawsuit for dissolution of the company on the grounds that its rights to know, right to claim profit distribution, etc. are impaired, or that the company is at a loss, its property is insufficient to repay all debts, or the company's business license as a legal person has been revoked without liquidation, the People's Court shall not accept the case.

Article 7: The company shall, in accordance with Article 183 of the Company Law, establish a liquidation group within fifteen days from the date the cause for dissolution arises and commence voluntary liquidation.
Where any of the following circumstances exists, and a creditor applies to the People's Court to appoint a liquidation group for liquidation, the People's Court shall accept the application:
(1) The company fails to establish a liquidation group for liquidation after the expiration of the time limit for dissolution;
(2) A liquidation group has been established but intentionally delays the liquidation;
(3) The illegal liquidation may seriously damage the interests of creditors or shareholders.
Where the circumstances listed in paragraph 2 exist and the creditor fails to file a liquidation application, and the company's shareholder applies to the People's Court to appoint a liquidation group for liquidation of the company, the People's Court shall accept the application.

Article 8: After accepting a case of company liquidation, the People's Court shall timely appoint relevant personnel to form a liquidation group.
Members of the liquidation group may be selected from the following persons or institutions:
(1) Shareholders, directors, supervisors, or senior officers of the company;
(2) Social intermediary institutions lawfully established, such as law firms, accounting firms, and bankruptcy liquidation firms;
(3) Individuals with relevant professional knowledge and practicing qualifications from social intermediary institutions lawfully established, such as law firms, accounting firms, and bankruptcy liquidation firms.

Article 9: Where a member of the liquidation group appointed by the People's Court falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may, upon application by a creditor or shareholder, or ex officio, replace the liquidation group member:
(1) Having acted in violation of laws or administrative regulations;
(2) Losing the capacity to practice or capacity for civil conduct;
(3) Having acted to seriously damage the interests of the company or creditors.

Article 11 Where a contributor makes a capital contribution with equity interests of another company and satisfies the following conditions, the People's Court shall hold that the contributor has performed its capital contribution obligation:
(1) The contributed equity interests are lawfully held by the contributor and may be transferred according to law;
(2) The contributed equity interests are free from defects in rights or encumbrances;
(3) The contributor has completed the statutory procedures for the transfer of equity interests;
(4) The contributed equity interests have been valued according to law.
Where the contribution of equity interests does not comply with items (1), (2), or (3) of the preceding paragraph, and the company, other shareholders, or creditors of the company request a determination that the contributor has not performed its capital contribution obligation, the People's Court shall order the contributor to take remedial measures within a specified reasonable period to meet the aforesaid conditions; if the contributor fails to remedy within the time limit, the People's Court shall find that the contributor has not fully performed its capital contribution obligation in accordance with law.
Where the contribution of equity interests does not comply with item (4) of the first paragraph of this Article, and the company, other shareholders, or creditors of the company request a determination that the contributor has not performed its capital contribution obligation, the People's Court shall handle the matter in accordance with Article 9 of these Provisions.

Article 12 After the incorporation of a company, where the company, shareholders, or creditors of the company request a determination that a relevant shareholder has withdrawn its capital contribution on the grounds that the shareholder's conduct falls under any of the following circumstances and harms the company's interests, the People's Court shall support such request:
(1) Fabricating false financial accounting statements to inflate profits for distribution;
(2) Transferring out its capital contribution by fabricating creditor-debtor relationships;
(3) Transferring out its capital contribution through affiliated transactions;
(4) Other acts of withdrawing capital contribution without statutory procedures.

Article 22 Where a dispute arises between parties as to the ownership of equity interests, and one party requests the People's Court to confirm that it holds the equity interests, it shall prove one of the following facts:
(1) It has already made a capital contribution to the company or subscribed for capital contribution in accordance with law, and such act does not violate mandatory provisions of laws or regulations;
(2) It has already acquired the company's equity interests by transfer or by other forms of succession, and such acquisition does not violate mandatory provisions of laws or regulations.
